{ Original Hippopotamus as Black course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Nf3 a6 5. Bd3 b6 6. O-O Bb7 7. Re1 e6 8. a4 Nd7 9. h3 h6 10. Be3 Ne7 11. Qd2 { Laboratory starts here. This illustrative route shows the recognizable formation when White develops quietly. The knights avoid blocking the fianchetto bishops, but Black has used many pawn tempi and is still uncastled. Decide on king safety and a central break. Examine ...c5 against d4 and ...d5 against e4; neither move is compulsory or safe in every version. } 11... Nf6 { Candidate comparison. Develops the king knight toward e4 and d5. Its location also blocks some long-diagonal bishop attacks, but makes it a possible target of e5. } 12. Rad1 d5 13. Bf4 Nxe4 14. Nxe4 dxe4 15. Bxe4 Bxe4 { Candidate comparison. The bishop captures a bishop on e4. Calculate the best reply before assuming a recapture, then recount material and opened lines. } 16. Rxe4 Nd5 17. Be5 O-O 18. c4 Nf6 19. Rh4 g5 { Candidate comparison. Places the pawn on g5, attacking rook on h4. A legal capture may still be unsound if the target is defended. } 20. Nxg5 hxg5 21. Qxg5 *

