{ Original Hippopotamus as Black course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. c4 d6 4. Nc3 e6 5. Nf3 Ne7 6. Be2 O-O 7. O-O h6 8. Re1 b6 9. Be3 f5 10. exf5 Nxf5 11. Bd3 { Laboratory starts here. White occupies c4-d4-e4. Black chooses a partial formation and castles instead of insisting on both fianchettos before addressing the center. Prepare ...c5 or ...d5 with enough support. A bishop on b7 is useful only if its diagonal and the required tempi justify it. } 11... Nd7 { Candidate comparison. Develops a knight toward central supporting squares. Keep a useful onward route so the compact setup does not become overcrowded. } 12. Rc1 Bb7 13. d5 e5 14. Bb1 a5 15. a3 Nf6 { Candidate comparison. Develops the king knight toward e4 and d5. Its location also blocks some long-diagonal bishop attacks, but makes it a possible target of e5. } 16. h3 Bc8 17. Bd2 Bd7 18. Qe2 Nh5 19. Nb5 Ne7 { Candidate comparison. Develops the king knight while keeping the f-pawn flexible. It may support a central break or move toward g6 or c6 if safe. } 20. Nfd4 Nf4 21. Bxf4 *

