{ Original Hippopotamus as Black course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. h4 h5 5. Bg5 Nf6 6. Qd2 c6 7. a4 Nbd7 8. Be2 Qa5 9. Nf3 O-O 10. O-O Re8 11. Bh6 { Laboratory starts here. ...h5 stops the immediate h4-h5 lever. Black accepts a fixed kingside structure rather than allowing White to open the h-file by force. Develop while watching g4 and the central breaks. Keep the king flexible until you know whether the h-file will open. } 11... e5 { Candidate comparison. Changes central space and pawn contacts. Calculate captures, advances and maintaining tension before choosing the next piece plan. } 12. Bxg7 Kxg7 13. Ng5 Re7 14. dxe5 dxe5 15. Rfb1 Qc7 { Candidate comparison. Places the queen on c7. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 16. a5 Nf8 17. b4 Ne6 18. Nxe6+ Bxe6 19. Qe3 Nd7 { Candidate comparison. Develops a knight toward central supporting squares. Keep a useful onward route so the compact setup does not become overcrowded. } 20. Rd1 Ree8 21. Rd2 *

