{ Original Hippopotamus as Black course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. d4 g6 2. Nf3 Bg7 3. Bf4 d6 4. e3 b6 5. h3 Bb7 6. Be2 e6 7. O-O Nd7 8. c4 Ngf6 9. Nfd2 e5 10. Bg3 exd4 11. exd4 { Laboratory starts here. White's bishop develops outside the pawn chain and may support e5. Black uses the double fianchetto while keeping the central break flexible. Develop the king knight and decide between ...c5 and ...e5. Count the support of e5 before allowing a white knight or pawn there. } 11... O-O { Candidate comparison. Moves the king toward shelter and brings a rook toward the center. Check the resulting open lines before assuming the king is safe. } 12. Nc3 Re8 13. Bh4 h6 14. Rc1 a6 15. a3 Nf8 { Candidate comparison. Places the knight on f8. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 16. f4 Ne6 17. d5 Nd4 18. Bd3 Qd7 19. f5 g5 { Candidate comparison. Places the pawn on g5, attacking bishop on h4. A legal capture may still be unsound if the target is defended. } 20. Be1 Re5 21. Bf2 *

