{ Original Hippopotamus as Black course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Nf3 e6 5. Be2 Ne7 6. O-O O-O 7. Be3 Nd7 8. Qd2 a6 9. a4 b6 10. Rfe1 Bb7 11. Bg5 { Laboratory starts here. Black develops the queen knight before forcing a central break. In this exact position an immediate ...c5 allows strong white play; a thematic move still needs preparation. Compare ...c5 and ...d5 on the next move after checking White's reply. If ...c5 becomes safe, calculate dxc5 and d5 separately and identify the opened file. } 11... Kh8 { Candidate comparison. Places the king on h8. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 12. Bc4 Nf6 13. Rad1 Qd7 14. h3 Nfg8 15. Qe2 f6 { Candidate comparison. Places the pawn on f6, attacking bishop on g5. A legal capture may still be unsound if the target is defended. } 16. Bc1 g5 17. Nh2 Ng6 18. Qg4 f5 19. exf5 exf5 { Candidate comparison. The pawn captures a pawn on f5. Calculate the best reply before assuming a recapture, then recount material and opened lines. } 20. Qh5 Rae8 21. Rxe8 *

