{ Original Hippopotamus as Black course. Study material, not a historical game. } 1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 a6 5. Qd2 Nf6 6. Bh6 Bxh6 7. Qxh6 b6 8. e5 Nfd7 9. Nf3 e6 10. O-O-O dxe5 11. Bc4 { Laboratory starts here. Black develops Nf6 before this bishop-exchange sequence. White removes the fianchetto bishop and places the queen near the king side, creating a real dark-square concern. Develop with attention to king safety and central counterplay. Choose the king's destination from the concrete threats instead of castling automatically toward the queen. } 11... Bb7 { Candidate comparison. Places the bishop on b7, attacking knight on f3. A legal capture may still be unsound if the target is defended. } 12. d5 Qe7 13. Rhe1 b5 14. Bb3 f6 15. dxe6 Nb6 { Candidate comparison. Places the knight on b6. Compare its activity and safety with its previous square, then use the branch explanation to connect the move to a plan. } 16. h4 Nc6 17. h5 Qf8 18. Qe3 Rd8 19. Ne4 gxh5 { Candidate comparison. The pawn captures a pawn on h5. Calculate the best reply before assuming a recapture, then recount material and opened lines. } 20. Nc5 Bc8 21. Kb1 *

